There are actually a few separate bugs stacked on top of each other here, not just one:

1. Wrong operator — nums[i] / 2 == 0 is integer division, not a remainder check. It's only true when nums[i] is 0 or 1, not when it's even. You want the modulo operator: nums[i] % 2 == 0.
2. Variable name mismatch — you declare ArrayList anwer (misspelled) but then reference answer (spelled correctly) inside the loop. Java will fail to compile because answer is never declared.
3. Missing closing parenthesis — answer.add(nums[i] is missing the closing ) for the add(...) call.
4. Missing return statement — the method is declared to return a List, but there's no return anywhere, so even once it compiles it won't give you anything back.

Here's a corrected version:

public static List<Integer> checkForEvens(int[] nums) {
    ArrayList<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<Integer>();
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (nums[i] % 2 == 0) {
            answer.add(nums[i]);
        }
    }
    return answer;
}

That should compile and actually collect the even numbers into answer and return them.

In discord.js (v14), you register event listeners on the client with .on() for repeating events and .once() for one-time events like startup:

const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, Events } = require('discord.js');

const client = new Client({
  intents: [GatewayIntentBits.Guilds, GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages, GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent],
});

client.once(Events.ClientReady, (c) => {
  console.log(`Logged in as ${c.user.tag}`);
});

client.on(Events.MessageCreate, (message) => {
  if (message.author.bot) return;
  console.log(`Message received: ${message.content}`);
});

client.login('your-token-here');

A couple of things that trip people up: you need to declare the right intents when creating the client, or events like message content just won't fire. Also, use the Events enum (Events.MessageCreate) rather than raw strings like 'messageCreate' — it's equivalent, but gives you autocomplete and avoids typos. And ready should almost always be registered with once, since it only fires the first time the client connects.
